ous.\n\nComparing T4 and T7: T4 is a middle tile, more dangerous because it can be used in many chows. T7 is a terminal-ish but 7 can be used in chows 567, 678, 789. But given many middle tiles are out? Unshown T4: 3 left, T7: 4 left. Both have many left.\n\nMaybe choose based on tile efficiency: If I discard T7, then my hand becomes: chows W123, W567, T345, pair B7, singles T4, B5. Then I need either to pair T4 or B5, or form a chow using T4 and B5 with another tile. That's still one away. If I discard B5, then singles T4, T7, pair B7. Need chow using T4 and T7? Not likely. If I discard T4, then singles T7, B5, pair B7. Need chow using T7 and B5? Not likely. So all similar.\n\nMaybe consider the wait after discarding: I will be waiting for a tile to complete either a pair or a chow. If I discard T7, I have two singles T4 and B5. I could wait for T4 or B5 to pair, or for tiles to form chows like T4 with T2,T3,T4 or T4,T5,T6 (but T5 used), or B5 with B3,B4,B5 or B5,B6,B7. That gives multiple possible draws. If I discard T4, I wait for T7 or B5 to pair, or chows for T7 or B5. If I discard B5, wait for T4 or T7 to pair. All similar.\n\nBut note: I have a pair of B7, so if I draw B6, I could have chow B5-B6-B7? But B5 is a single. If I keep B5 and discard T7, then drawing B6 gives me chow B5-B6-B7, which uses my B7 pair, breaking the pair. Then I would need a new pair. That's okay because I would then have 4 chows and a single (T4), needing a pair for T4. So that's a possibility. If I discard B5, then drawing B6 doesn't help. So keeping B5 might be good for that chow possibility. Similarly, keeping T4 might allow chow T2-T3-T4 or T4-T5-T6 if I draw T2 or T6, but T3 and T5 are used, so T2-T3-T4 would require using T3 from existing chow, which might be okay if I rearrange. But that's more complicated.\n\nGiven simplicity, I think discard T7 is fine.\n\nAlso look at opponents' discards: Next player discarded many winds, so they might not need suited tiles. Across player has a pung of F4, and discarded T8, W7, B2, T5, F2. So they discarded T5 and T8, maybe they are discarding T suit tiles. So T7 might be safe because they didn't keep T5 or T8. Prev player discarded many winds and B9. So T7 might be relatively safe.\n\nTherefore, I'll discard T7.\n\nReason: My hand is close to a winning shape with three chows and a pair. Discarding the isolated T7 keeps the chow structure intact and avoids breaking potential sequences. It also seems relatively safe based on opponents' discards.\n\nAnswer: Play T7"}
