air from W5 or W6 or T2 or T3, and meld from others. But that's messy.  \n\nBetter to keep tenpai and hope for self-drawn plus other patterns? But patterns too low.  \n\nMaybe discard T3: then hand: W5,W6,W7,T2. Then I need a pair (could be any of these tiles) and a meld? Actually, W5,W6,W7 is a meld, so leftover T2 needs a pair T2. So still waiting for T2. Same as before but lost T3 wait. So worse.  \n\nDiscard T2: then waiting for T3 only.  \n\nDiscard W5: hand: W6,W7,T2,T3. Then possible melds: W6,W7 with W8 or W5 for chow, or T2,T3 with T4 or T1 for chow. Could aim for mixed shifted chows etc. But exposed melds limit.  \n\nGiven the situation, maybe the safest is to keep tenpai and hope for a high-point win later by drawing a suitable tile before winning? But if I draw a non-winning tile, I must discard.  \n\nGiven the game asks for discard now, I must choose one. I'll choose the least useful tile for future flexibility. T2 and T3 are my winning tiles, so keep them. Among W5,W6,W7, discarding W7 keeps W5,W6 which can pair with another W5 or W6, and can form chow with W4/W7 or W6/W7? Actually W5,W6 can form chow with W4,W5,W6 or W5,W6,W7 or W6,W7,W8. So more flexibility. Discarding W5 or W6 similar.  \n\nBut discarding W7 breaks the ready chow, but maybe I can form a different chow.  \n\nGiven the unseen tiles: W4: 3 left, W8: 4 left. So if I discard W7, I can still use W5,W6 with W4 or W8 to form chow.  \n\nAlso, T2 and T3 can pair or form chow with T1/T4.  \n\nSo maybe discard W7 is okay.  \n\nBut let's check opponents' discards: next player history shows many honors discarded, across player has pung F4, previous player discarded many winds. So winds are being discarded. Maybe I could aim for a wind pair? But I have no winds.  \n\nGiven my hand is simple, maybe just go for quick win. But need 8 points. Perhaps if I win by self-drawn with a closed wait or edge wait, plus no honors, plus all simples? But all simples not satisfied due to W1.  \n\nWait, W1 is terminal, so not all simples. So all simples not possible.  \n\nThus, my current wait is low value. I need to change hand to get at least 8 points. That means I need to form a high-value pattern. Given exposed melds, hard. Maybe aim for \"All Types\" by getting a wind/dragon pair and a dragon pung? But I have no dragons/winds now.  \n\nMaybe aim for \"Mixed Triple Chow\" \u2014 but my exposed chows are different suits.  \n\nGiven the difficulty, perhaps the best is to discard T2 or T3 to break the low-value wait and try to build a better hand. But that means starting over.  \n\nGiven the game stage (many tiles discarded), maybe better to keep tenpai and hope for self-drawn plus last tile or other opportunity.  \n\nI'll choose to discard W7, keeping W5,W6,T2,T3, still waiting for T2 or T3, but also now can use W4/W8 with W5,W6 for new chow possibilities, increasing flexibility.  \n\nReason: W7 is less connected than W5 or W6 because W5,W6 can connect to both W4 and W7/W8, while W7 only connects to W5,W6,W8,W9. But with W5,W6 kept, better shape.  \n\nAlso, discarding W7 avoids potential conflict if I draw W8 later and want to change wait.  \n\nSo final choice: Play W7.  \n\nEvaluation: Current hand is in tenpai waiting for T2 or T3, but likely worth less than 8 points. Discarding W7 maintains tenpai (still wait for T2/T3) while slightly improving flexibility for future draws to shift to a higher-value wait.  \n\nReason: W7 is the least flexible among the W tiles to form new combinations without breaking the existing wait, and keeping W5,W6 offers more chow possibilities. Given the need for at least 8 points to win, maintaining tenpai while hoping for a self-drawn win with additional point patterns (e.g., Self-Drawn, Closed Wait, etc.) may be viable, but discarding W7 does not immediately improve point value. However, among the options, discarding W7 is safe and keeps the hand ready.  \n\nAnswer: Play W7"}
